            {
                "node": {
                    "created_at": "1773278009",
                    "text": "To disprove the myth that piranhas are instant “crusher machines,” biologist and presenter Jeremy Wade conducted one of the most famous experiments on the River Monsters program.\n\nHe got into a pool full of red-bellied piranhas (Pygocentrus nattereri), which had been deprived of food, to show that they do not attack humans without a specific stimulus, such as blood or a frantic movement of agony.\n\nWade showed that the predatory behavior of these fish is actually opportunistic and defensive. In the wild, piranhas act as “cleaners” of rivers, feeding on carron or injured animals.\n\nThe collective panic around them has historically been fueled by exaggerated accounts, including a visit by Theodore Roosevelt to Brazil. Wade’s experiment showed that, in the absence of blood in the water or extreme drought conditions, when food is scarce and aggressiveness increases, they prefer to keep their distance from a large and calm animal like humans.\n\nIn this account you learn every day something you didn’t know... or\n\nFollow me on @unexplained_case\n\nTo share 🌎 \n\nLike if you liked it ❤️ \n\nI read your comments 👀",
                    "id": "18079495268098076"
                }
            }
        ]
